AIM:

To design and develop a RESTful API for Product Management using Spring Boot by implementing HTTP methods such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE, and to return responses in JSON format. The APIs are tested using a REST client.


PROCEDURE:
Create a Spring Boot project in Eclipse
Add dependencies:
Spring Web
Spring Data JPA (optional for DB)
Create Product class (model)
Create REST Controller using @RestController
Implement CRUD operations:
GET → retrieve products
POST → add product
PUT → update product
DELETE → remove product
Store data in memory (List)
Run the application
Test APIs using Postman


CODE:
1. Product.java
package com.example.product;

public class Product {

    private int id;
    private String name;
    private double price;

    public Product() {}

    public Product(int id, String name, double price) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
    }

    public int getId() { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
    public double getPrice() { return price; }

    public void setId(int id) { this.id = id; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }
    public void setPrice(double price) { this.price = price; }
}

2. ProductController.java
package com.example.product;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/products")
public class ProductController {

    private List<Product> productList = new ArrayList<>();

    // GET - Read all products
    @GetMapping
    public List<Product> getAllProducts() {
        return productList;
    }

    // POST - Add product
    @PostMapping
    public String addProduct(@RequestBody Product p) {
        productList.add(p);
        return "Product added successfully";
    }

    // PUT - Update product
    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public String updateProduct(@PathVariable int id, @RequestBody Product p) {
        for (Product prod : productList) {
            if (prod.getId() == id) {
                prod.setName(p.getName());
                prod.setPrice(p.getPrice());
                return "Product updated";
            }
        }
        return "Product not found";
    }

    // DELETE - Remove product
    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    public String deleteProduct(@PathVariable int id) {
        Iterator<Product> it = productList.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            Product p = it.next();
            if (p.getId() == id) {
                it.remove();
                return "Product deleted";
            }
        }
        return "Product not found";
    }
}

3. Main Application
package com.example.product;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;

@SpringBootApplication
public class ProductApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(ProductApplication.class, args);
    }
}


RESULT:

Thus, a RESTful API for Product Management was successfully developed using Spring Boot.
All CRUD operations (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) were implemented and returned JSON responses.
The APIs were tested successfully using a REST client, and the expected results were obtained.
